\subsection{Whats going on with the flip from syn to asyn just by adding burnin time only for logis}
In cell 1,1. Sp2 steadily grows - just from population dynamics. The two species are stabilizing towards coexistence equilibrium.
When this cell reaches high litter status, sp2 jumps up.
At the next time point, this is too much for sp1 to handle so it goes down. 
As litter declines, sp2 starts leaking out and sp1 climbs again.
When sp2 is low, sp1 is climbing towards its monoculture equilibrium. Sp1 in that cell has to steadily climb until it becomes the litter champion for sp2 to come in again.


When theres no burnin time, both species are boosted by litter and hurt by less litter. 

\subsection{They want to be synchronous so bad}
So - I have a wholy grail result. And that wholy grail result occurs when 
1) theres a sufficient burn in time (>3), 
2) theres low litter accumulation (~<0.15),
3) theta is high. 

When these things diverge away from wholy grail parameters, 
synchrony comes back. And not just any kind of synchrony.
Its a strong switch back year to year. 
Time adjacent peaks and valleys in sp2 as its tracking litter.
Even when I change theta, which doesnt directly alter the amount of litter...
Low theta makes the change.. 

Whens there's alot of sp1 at t=10, litter is high at t=11, and sp2 is boosted in t=12.


