\section{Non-dimensionalization of the Discrete-Time Lotka-Volterra Competition Model (without germination)}\label{section:ndm deriv}

We begin with the standard discrete-time Lotka-Volterra competition model for two species:

\begin{align}
n_{1,t+1} &= n_{1,t} \cdot \lambda_1 \cdot \left(1 - \alpha_{11} n_{1,t} - \alpha_{12} n_{2,t} \right), \\
n_{2,t+1} &= n_{2,t} \cdot \lambda_2 \cdot \left(1 - \alpha_{22} n_{2,t} - \alpha_{21} n_{1,t} \right),
\end{align}

where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $n_{i,t}$ is the population size of species $i$ at time $t$,
  \item $\lambda_i$ is the finite rate of increase for species $i$,
  \item $\alpha_{ij}$ is the per capita competitive effect of species $j$ on species $i$.
\end{itemize}

We non-dimensionalize the system by rescaling population sizes relative to the intraspecific carrying capacities:

\[
K_1 = \frac{1}{\alpha_{11}}, \qquad K_2 = \frac{1}{\alpha_{22}},
\]
\[
x_t = \frac{n_{1,t}}{K_1}, \qquad y_t = \frac{n_{2,t}}{K_2}.
\]

Substituting into the original equations and simplifying, we obtain:

%\begin{align*}
%x_{t+1} &= x_t \cdot \lambda_1 \cdot \left(1 - x_t - \beta_{12} y_t \right%), \\
%y_{t+1} &= y_t \cdot \lambda_2 \cdot \left(1 - y_t - \beta_{21} x_t \right%),
%\end{align*}
%change from beta12 -> alpha nad beta21 -> beta

\begin{align*}
x_{t+1} &= x_t \cdot \lambda_1 \cdot \left(1 - x_t - \alpha \cdot y_t \right), \\
y_{t+1} &= y_t \cdot \lambda_2 \cdot \left(1 - y_t - \beta \cdot x_t \right),
\end{align*}

where:
\[
\alpha = \frac{\alpha_{12}}{\alpha_{22}}, \qquad \beta = \frac{\alpha_{21}}{\alpha_{11}}.
\]

This system is now expressed in terms of dimensionless abundances $x_t$ and $y_t \in [0,1]$, scaled by each species' carrying capacity. 
The parameters %$\beta_{ij}$ 
$\alpha$ and $\beta$ represent the strength of interspecific competition relative to intraspecific competition.

$x$ and $y$ are replaced with $N_1$ and $N_2$ in previous sections.

\subsection{Equilibrium Analysis}

To find equilibrium points, we solve for fixed points $(x^*, y^*)$ where $x_{t+1} = x_t = x^*$ and $y_{t+1} = y_t = y^*$. Substituting into the model:

\begin{align*}
x^* &= x^* \cdot \lambda_1 (1 - x^* - \alpha y^*), \\
y^* &= y^* \cdot \lambda_2 (1 - y^* - \beta x^*).
\end{align*}

Factoring gives:

\begin{align*}
x^* \left[\lambda_1 (1 - x^* - \alpha y^*) - 1 \right] &= 0, \\
y^* \left[\lambda_2 (1 - y^* - \beta x^*) - 1 \right] &= 0.
\end{align*}

This yields four possible equilibrium types:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Extinction}: $(x^*, y^*) = (0, 0)$.
  \item \textbf{Species 1 only}: if $\lambda_1 > 1$, then
  \[
  (x^*, y^*) = \left(1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_1}, 0\right).
  \]
  \item \textbf{Species 2 only}: if $\lambda_2 > 1$, then
  \[
  (x^*, y^*) = \left(0, 1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_2} \right).
  \]
  \item \textbf{Coexistence} (both species present): solve
  \[
  \lambda_1 (1 - x^* - \alpha y^*) = 1, \qquad \lambda_2 (1 - y^* - \beta x^*) = 1.
  \]
  Solving the first equation for $x^*$:
  \[
  x^* = 1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_1} - \alpha y^*,
  \]
  and substituting into the second:
  \[
  y^* = \frac{1 - \beta(1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_1}) - \frac{1}{\lambda_2}}{1 + \beta \alpha}.
  \]
  Then back-substitute to get $x^*$.
\end{itemize}

Coexistence requires $x^* > 0$ and $y^* > 0$, which occurs when interspecific competition is sufficiently weak and both species have $\lambda_i > 1$.

